<context>
Epic EP-001 — Calendar clones through Calendar.app.
Goal: every calendar the Operator owns shows the busy slots of the other calendars, so colleagues on any account
see the real availability and the Operator gets notified on every client.
Architecture: a single Swift executable (`calsyn-cli`, new Swift Package `calsyn`) talks to the calendar store
through EventKit and never touches credentials.

Scope of this workflow:
- TASK-001: `calsyn once` reads `~/.config/calsyn/config.yaml` (or the file given with `--config`), validates it,
  logs the outcome and exits with a code that tells success from failure. Components: cli, config-loader, runner.
- TASK-002: `calsyn calendars` prints one row per calendar with account and title, ready to copy into
  `config.yaml`. Components: cli, calendar-store.
</context>
<input type="task">alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-001.md</input>
<input type="task">alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-002.md</input>
<coding_test>
- `swift test`: config-loader on valid and invalid fixtures (AS-01, AS-02); run on every change.
- `swift test`: log formatter output (AS-17); run on every change.
- Manual E2E: `calsyn calendars` on a Mac with Exchange + Google accounts (AS-03); before closing TASK-002.
- Manual E2E: revoke access in System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Calendars, run `calsyn calendars` (AS-13); before closing TASK-002.
</coding_test>
<available_agents>
    <agent subagent_type="alligator-llm:developer" id="developer">Implements the code of the tasks and applies the fixes requested by the reviewer.</agent>
    <agent subagent_type="alligator-llm:devils-advocate" id="devils-advocate">Reviews the implementation against the tasks' Definition of Done and the epic's architecture, and reports the gaps to the developer.</agent>
</available_agents>
<tasks>
    <task id="task-001">
        `developer` implements TASK-001. Definition of Done:
        - `calsyn once` with a valid config logs `msg="settings loaded"` with the number of calendars and jobs and exits with code 0
        - `calsyn once --config &lt;path&gt;` reads the given file instead of `~/.config/calsyn/config.yaml`
        - A config with a missing required field logs an ERROR line naming the field, exits with a code ≠ 0 and writes no calendar
        - A job whose `source` or `destinations` reference a `name` not declared in `calendars` logs an ERROR line naming it, exits with a code ≠ 0 and writes no calendar
        - `details.title` and `details.description` default to false when omitted
        - Every line written by calsyn is logfmt with level INFO or ERROR
        `devils-advocate` reviews the result; `developer` applies the fixes until the review passes.
    </task>
    <task id="task-002">
        `developer` implements TASK-002. Definition of Done:
        - `calsyn calendars` prints one row per calendar visible in Calendar.app, showing account and title
        - On first use macOS asks the Operator to grant Calendar access to calsyn
        - With Calendar access denied, any calsyn command that reads calendars logs an ERROR line and exits with a code ≠ 0
        - calsyn never asks for nor stores account credentials
        `devils-advocate` reviews the result; `developer` applies the fixes until the review passes.
    </task>
    <task id="close-specs">
        Rename `alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-001.md` to `alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-001-done.md` and
        `alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-002.md` to `alligator/specs/EP-001/TASK-002-done.md`.
        Update every reference to the old names under `alligator/specs/`.
    </task>
</tasks>
